            <ArticleTitle>Indications and outcomes of reconstructive surgery in chronic pain of spinal origin.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>2555-62; discussion 2563</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="STUDY DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Peer-reviewed literature was reviewed and summarized.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To synthesize the indications and published outcomes of reconstructive lumbar spine surgery for the treatment of chronic pain of spinal origin.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">A literature review was conducted.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The most common indication for reconstructive lumbar surgery is pain that is refractory to nonsurgical treatment. Lumbar fusion has been shown to improve symptoms in carefully selected patients with incapacitating pain.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">A successful arthrodesis is the fundamental surgical goal for patients with chronic pain of spinal origin. However, a successful fusion does not always correlate with a successful clinical result.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
